Video Outline: Combinators — Descendant, Child, Adjacent Sibling & General Sibling
Topic: CSS Combinators & Selector Relationships
Duration: Max 5 minutes
Subject: Cascading Style Sheets (CSS)
 
Introduction — 0:00–0:45
Hook: CSS selectors don’t just target elements alone — they target elements based on their relationship to others
What are Combinators? Symbols that describe the connection between selectors; let you style elements by their position in the HTML structure
4 Types we’ll cover: Descendant, Child, Adjacent Sibling, General Sibling
Goal: By the end, you’ll know exactly when & how to use each one
Concept Explanation — 0:45–2:30
A. Descendant Selector (space)
Selects all matching elements inside a parent — at any depth
Syntax:  parent child { } 
Example:  div p { }  → all  
  inside any  
 
B. Child Selector ( > )
Selects only direct children — one level down
Syntax:  parent > child { } 
Example:  div > p { }  → only  
  directly inside  
 ; not nested deeper
C. Adjacent Sibling Selector ( + )
Selects the immediately following sibling — same parent, right next
Syntax:  element + sibling { } 
Example:  h2 + p { }  → only the first  
  right after  
 
D. General Sibling Selector ( ~ )
Selects all matching siblings that come after — same parent, anywhere below
Syntax:  element ~ sibling { } 
Example:  h2 ~ p { }  → every  
  that follows  
  under the same parent
